@model CatalogOverview
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About the data";
    var complaintsInCatalogue = Model.ComplaintsInCatalogue;
    var complaintsWithoutModelYear = Model.Complaints - complaintsInCatalogue;
    var makesOutsideCatalogue = Model.CanonicalMakes - Model.Makes;
}

@section Styles {
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/pages/recalls.css" asp-append-version="true" />
}

<header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">About the data</p>
    <h1>What this data does and does not say</h1>
    <p class="page-head__meta">
        Garage Doctor reads one public dataset closely. Reading it closely also means being
        clear about where it stops being trustworthy.
    </p>
</header>

<div class="stat-row campaign-figures">
    <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat__value">@Formats.Number(Model.Complaints)</div>
        <div class="stat__label">Vehicle complaints</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat__value">@Formats.Number(Model.Recalls)</div>
        <div class="stat__label">Recall rows</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat__value">@Formats.Number(Model.VehicleCombinations)</div>
        <div class="stat__label">Make, model and year</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
        <div class="stat__value">@Formats.Number(Model.Makes)</div>
        <div class="stat__label">Makes in the catalogue</div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="doc">
    <section>
        <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Everything here is built from the flat files published by the
                <a href="https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/ffdd/" rel="noreferrer">NHTSA Office of Defects Investigation</a>,
                the defect investigation arm of the US National Highway Traffic Safety
                Administration. The files are refreshed daily and released as <span class="mono">us-pd</span>,
                public domain, with no usage conditions attached.
            </p>
            <p>
                The figures above are counted from the live database rather than quoted from a
                readme, so they move when the data is reloaded. The complaints run from 1995
                onward and the recall files reach back to 1967. Model years present in the
                catalogue span
                @if (Model.EarliestModelYear.HasValue && Model.LatestModelYear.HasValue)
                {
                    <text>@Model.EarliestModelYear to @Model.LatestModelYear.</text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text>the full range of the source files.</text>
                }
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Complaints are not failure rates</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                This is the single most important thing on the page. A complaint is filed
                voluntarily by a vehicle owner who chose to contact a federal agency about a
                fault. Nobody is sampled and nobody is surveyed.
            </p>
            <p>
                A model sold in large numbers therefore collects more complaints than a rare one
                regardless of how well it was built, and a fault that frightens people gets
                reported far more often than one that merely annoys them. Nothing in this
                application is normalized by fleet size, because NHTSA does not publish how many
                of each model are on the road. Read the component breakdowns as a picture of what
                owners of a given car complain about, not as a ranking of which car is worse.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Mileage is present in about half the records</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                The mileage field is optional and frequently left blank:
                @Formats.Number(Model.ComplaintsWithMileage) of @Formats.Number(Model.Complaints)
                complaints carry one, which is @Formats.Share(Model.ComplaintsWithMileage, Model.Complaints)
                of the file.
            </p>
            <p>
                That is why every mileage histogram in the application prints its own sample size
                next to it instead of quietly plotting a subset as though it were the whole. A
                histogram drawn from fifty records looks exactly like one drawn from five
                thousand unless it tells you which it is.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Not every complaint belongs to a vehicle profile</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                A profile in this application is defined per make, model and model year, so a
                complaint has to carry all three to be attached to one. Not all of them do:
                @Formats.Number(complaintsWithoutModelYear) of the
                @Formats.Number(Model.Complaints) vehicle complaints have no usable model year,
                because the source file either leaves the year field empty or fills it with
                <span class="mono">9999</span>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The browsable catalogue therefore covers @Formats.Number(complaintsInCatalogue)
                complaints across @Formats.Number(Model.VehicleCombinations) make, model and year
                combinations, and lists @Formats.Number(Model.Makes) makes against the @Formats.Number(Model.CanonicalMakes)
                canonical makes that exist across all complaints. This is a design decision rather
                than a discrepancy. A report with no model year cannot honestly be assigned to one,
                and inventing a year to make the totals line up would be worse than the gap. Those
                records stay in the database and stay in the headline count above, they simply have
                no vehicle page to sit on.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>A recall row is not a recall campaign</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                One row in the recall file is a campaign, model and model-year combination, not a
                campaign. A single campaign covering twelve models across nine model years
                produces around a hundred rows.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is why Mercedes-Benz leads the recall file with 45 411 rows while sitting far
                down the complaint counts. It reflects an unusually fragmented model range, badged
                across many variants and years, not a quality signal. Any chart that ranks makes
                by raw recall rows is measuring model-range structure and calling it reliability.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>US market only</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                NHTSA regulates vehicles sold in the United States, so that is the entire
                population here. European engine variants largely do not appear, and makes that
                never sold in the US in volume are statistically absent, Renault, Peugeot and Opel
                among them.
            </p>
            <p>
                A European car with the same badge as an American one is often a different vehicle
                underneath. Treat the profiles as evidence about the US-market car.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Names had to be cleaned up, and the cleanup stops short</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                The raw file holds 1069 distinct make spellings. Normalization and a hand-written
                alias map fold them to @Formats.Number(Model.CanonicalMakes) canonical makes, merging cases like
                <span class="mono">MERCEDES</span>, <span class="mono">MERCEDES BENZ</span>
                and <span class="mono">MERCEDES-BENZ</span> into one.
            </p>
            <p>
                @Formats.Number(makesOutsideCatalogue) of those canonical makes never reach the browsable catalogue, for the
                reason set out above: every complaint filed against them lacks a model year, so
                there is no vehicle page for them to sit on.
            </p>
            <p>
                Canonicalization stops well short of tidy. Roughly 740 makes have fewer than ten
                complaints each and together account for a tenth of a percent of the data, but they
                are genuinely distinct trailer, bus, camper and motorcycle manufacturers rather
                than typos. Merging them by string similarity would corrupt the taxonomy to make a
                number look better, so they stay. Components got the same treatment from the other
                direction: a taxonomy that drifted over thirty years is folded into 26 canonical
                groups, with anything unmapped falling into a reported catch-all instead of
                quietly disappearing.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>What was deliberately thrown away</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Complaint narratives are free text written by members of the public, and people
                routinely type their own name, address or phone number into them. Eight source
                columns therefore never reach the database: the five dealer fields, the vehicle
                operator, the VIN and the reporter's city. The document type has no properties to
                hold them, and a test walks each stored document recursively and fails the build if
                any of those names appear at any depth.
            </p>
            <p>
                The repository holds no raw data either, only the download script, the field
                specifications and a manifest recording which snapshot was used. The dataset is
                public domain and republishing it would be permitted, but republishing other
                people's contact details is a different matter.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Use it accordingly</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                What this dataset is good for: seeing which parts of a specific car owners report
                trouble with, roughly when in the vehicle's life it happens, and which recall
                campaigns apply to it. What it cannot tell you is whether one car is more reliable
                than another. That question needs fleet numbers nobody publishes.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<p class="caveat">
    Source files and field specifications:
    <a href="https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/ffdd/" rel="noreferrer">static.nhtsa.gov/odi/ffdd</a>.
    Recall lookup by vehicle identification number:
    <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noreferrer">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a>.
    Nothing on this site is safety advice.
</p>
